wasi-sh: apply the "#!" rule to the PATH search too.

Applied AFTER ash-shebang.patch, whose rule this extends to the one place that
patch did not reach. That patch made `./vendor/bin/phpunit` runnable by reading
the "#!" line of a SLASHED name. A bare name goes down a different road —
find_command()'s PATH search — and that road ends at test_exec(), which cannot
succeed in this build: the shim FS carries permission bits, nothing enforces
them, WASI preview1's filestat has no mode field to report them through, and
there is no chmod applet to set one. Every file therefore stats as mode 0, and
`ls -l` shows ---------- for regular files and d--------- for directories.

So `composer` was EACCES while `./composer` ran — the same file, two answers,
and the difference visible only as "Permission denied" on a file the shell had
just written itself.

Two changes, both needed:

  1. find_command()'s PATH loop accepts a candidate that test_exec() rejected
     when it is a readable file whose first two bytes are "#!". Without this
     the search reports EACCES and prints its own message before anything
     downstream could rescue the command.

  2. shebang_expand() resolves a slashless name against PATH before reading it.
     find_command() does not keep the path it found, so this walks PATH again —
     only for a command that would otherwise have failed. It rewrites the
     command word to the resolved path, because that is what the interpreter
     must be handed: execve() passes the resolved path, and an interpreter
     given a bare name opens it relative to the cwd and fails.

Recursion is bounded by the depth cap ash-shebang.patch already enforces:
shebang_expand() refuses at depth > 0, so exactly one level is ever expanded
whatever the interpreter turns out to be. What changes is that a bare name can
now BE a "#!" file, which that patch's header calls structurally impossible;
the cap, not the impossibility, is what holds after this.

--- a/shell/ash.c
+++ b/shell/ash.c
--- a/shell/ash.c
+++ b/shell/ash.c
@@ -8818,6 +8818,30 @@
  * Resolve a command name.  If you change this routine, you may have to
  * change the shellexec routine as well.
  */
+/* wasi-sh: is PATH a file this shell is willing to run?
+ *
+ * The same rule ash-shebang.patch already applies to a slashed name -- A
+ * READABLE FILE WHOSE FIRST TWO BYTES ARE "#!" -- and it is here for the same
+ * reason: the shim FS carries permission bits but nothing enforces them, WASI's
+ * filestat has no mode field to report them through, and there is no chmod. So
+ * test_exec() answers no for every file that ever existed, and without this the
+ * PATH search rejects a perfectly good script with EACCES.
+ */
+static int
+is_shebang_file(const char *fullname)
+{
+	char two[2];
+	int fd;
+	ssize_t n;
+
+	fd = open(fullname, O_RDONLY);
+	if (fd < 0)
+		return 0;
+	n = read(fd, two, 2);
+	close(fd);
+	return n == 2 && two[0] == '#' && two[1] == '!';
+}
+
 static void
 find_command(char *name, struct cmdentry *entry, int act, const char *path)
 {
